<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    Nop.Admin - the administration UI, and the LARGEST SINGLE UI SURFACE in the
    application: 277 .cs files and 325 .cshtml views (design section 6).
    Downstream of Nop.Core, Nop.Data, Nop.Services and Nop.Web.Framework, and a
    parallelizable SIBLING of Nop.Web - neither project references the other, and there
    is no ProjectReference between them in either direction.
    (Requirements 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.5, 2.4, 4.11, 5.7, 5.10)

    SCOPE OF TASK 8.1: this file, the package graph and the project references ONLY.
    No .cs file, no .cshtml view, no AdminAreaRegistration.cs and no Web.config was
    touched - those are tasks 8.2 (area routing), 8.3 (controllers), 8.4 (views),
    8.5 (static assets), 8.6 (ImageSharp) and 8.7 (configuration / obsolete files).
    The project is EXPECTED not to compile at the end of 8.1; the surviving CS*/RZ*
    errors are the inventory those tasks consume, and 8.8 is the gate.

    ===================================================================================
    WHY Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web
    ===================================================================================
    Like Nop.Web and unlike Nop.Web.Framework (which has zero .cshtml files and stays on
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk - see the long note in that project), Nop.Admin owns 325 .cshtml
    views under Views\. The Web SDK imports the Razor SDK and supplies the ASP.NET Core
    app targets.

    NOTE: there is deliberately NO explicit <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
    below. Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web adds it implicitly and restating it fails the build with
    NETSDK1086 - the same trap task 7.1 recorded for Nop.Web. This is the one place the
    Nop.Web.Framework pattern must NOT be copied.

    THE TFM IS THE OS-AGNOSTIC net10.0, NOT net10.0-windows - and that is a deliberate
    decision with a cost attached, because this project is the ONLY one in the solution
    that genuinely binds the Windows-only System.Drawing imaging surface:
      Controllers\RoxyFilemanController.cs   Bitmap, Graphics, PixelFormat,
                                             InterpolationMode, Image.GetThumbnailImageAbort
    Design section 7 resolves that by REWRITING those call sites onto
    SixLabors.ImageSharp at task 8.6 rather than by pinning the whole project to Windows.
    The three ColorTranslator.FromHtml(...) colour-string validations in
    Controllers\ProductController.cs and Controllers\CheckoutAttributeController.cs are
    not an imaging pipeline at all and map onto SixLabors.ImageSharp.Color.TryParse.
    Until 8.6 lands, those five files are part of the CS* error inventory.

    ===================================================================================
    THE STRUCTURAL TRAP, SEEN FROM THIS SIDE
    ===================================================================================
    Nop.Admin does not live beside Nop.Web - it lives INSIDE it, at
    src\Presentation\Nop.Web\Administration\. Task 7.1 had to add
    <DefaultItemExcludes>$(DefaultItemExcludes);Administration\**;Plugins\**</DefaultItemExcludes>
    to Nop.Web.csproj so Nop.Web's implicit globs would not swallow this whole tree.

    The inverse needs NO exclusion, and that was verified by evaluating the resolved item
    lists rather than by reading globs. MSBuild roots the default globs at the directory
    containing the project file, so Nop.Admin's globs cannot reach UP into Nop.Web; and
    nothing is nested inside Administration\. Measured after conversion:
      Compile  277 items  (Models 127, Controllers 71, Validators 61, Extensions 8,
                           Infrastructure 3, AdminAreaRegistration.cs,
                           Properties\AssemblyInfo.cs, ...)   0 leakage
      Content  340 items  (325 .cshtml + 12 .json + 3 .config)  0 leakage
    277 is exactly the length of the legacy explicit <Compile> list, and 325 is exactly
    the .cshtml count on disk, so the globs reproduce the old compilation set precisely -
    no file silently gained or lost. Zero resolved item path escapes this directory.

    One consequence of the legacy layout is gone and is worth naming: the old project set
    <OutputPath>..\bin\</OutputPath>, i.e. it dropped Nop.Admin.dll straight into
    Nop.Web\bin as the MVC "Admin" area. That was a BUILD/DEPLOY relationship, not a
    compile-time reference (design section 6). The SDK writes to
    Administration\bin\Debug\net10.0\ instead, and task 8.2 re-expresses the hosting
    relationship as MapAreaControllerRoute contributed through IRouteProvider.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>

    <!--
      TASK 8.2 - REQUIRED, and its absence would fail the 8.8 gate with a single confusing error
      that has nothing to do with the ~3000 CS*/RZ* porting errors 8.1 inventoried.

      Nop.Admin is a LIBRARY that contributes an MVC area to the Nop.Web host. It is not an
      independently launchable application, has no Program.cs and never had a Global.asax (3.90
      recorded that as UseCustomServer=True with an external CustomServerUrl). But
      Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web defaults OutputType to Exe - MEASURED, not assumed:
      `dotnet msbuild Nop.Admin.csproj -getProperty:OutputType` returns "Exe" with this property
      removed - and an Exe with no Main gets

          CSC : error CS5001: Program does not contain a static 'Main' method suitable for an
          entry point

      which was observed on a throwaway Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web probe of this exact shape.

      NOTE ON WHEN IT BITES, because this was checked and the naive expectation was wrong:
      removing this property today does NOT produce CS5001, because Roslyn never reaches
      entry-point resolution while the compilation still has ~3000 DECLARATION-phase errors -
      the same phase-ordering behaviour recorded in runtime-deferrals.md section 19. The probe
      reproduced both sides: with only a method-BODY error present, CS5001 was reported
      alongside it; with declaration errors present, it was not. So CS5001 would have surfaced
      for the first time exactly when 8.3/8.4 finished and the gate was expected to pass.

      Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web is still the right SDK: it imports the Razor SDK, which is what
      compiles the 325 views under Areas/Admin/Views/.
    -->
    <OutputType>Library</OutputType>

    <!--
      ===================================================================================
      TASK 8.7 - REQUIRED, AND ITS ABSENCE IS A DEPLOYMENT-BREAKING DEFECT ON IIS.
      MEASURED, not reasoned about. DO NOT REMOVE.
      ===================================================================================
      Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web's publish pipeline runs the TransformWebConfig task, which
      injects the ASP.NET Core Module handler. That is correct for a HOST and wrong here:
      Nop.Admin is a LIBRARY (OutputType above), has no Program.cs and no Main, and is
      loaded by the Nop.Web host as an MVC area through an application part (task 8.2).

      MEASURED, before this property existed. `dotnet publish` of THIS project emitted an ANCM
      shim of its own, ~485 bytes:

            <handlers><add name="aspNetCore" ... modules="AspNetCoreModuleV2" /></handlers>
            <aspNetCore processPath="dotnet" arguments=".\Nop.Admin.dll"
                        hostingModel="inprocess" />

      i.e. it told IIS to launch Nop.Admin.dll as the application. There is no Main to
      launch.

      *** WHY THIS IS WORSE THAN A USELESS FILE: IT OVERWRITES THE STOREFRONT'S. ***
      Deferral 8.2-2, as narrowed by task 8.5 to deferral 8.5-1, records that the intended
      deployment is "publish Nop.Web, then publish Nop.Admin into the SAME directory".
      Measured on both sides:

          Nop.Web  publish -> web.config   14154 bytes  arguments=".\Nop.Web.dll"
                                           (7.4's merged file: ANCM handler PLUS the two
                                            live IIS elements, urlCompression and the
                                            X-Powered-By removal)
          Nop.Admin publish ->   485 bytes  arguments=".\Nop.Admin.dll"

      So publishing Nop.Admin second replaces a correct 14 KB storefront shim with a
      485-byte one that boots a library with no entry point, AND silently drops task 7.4's
      two IIS settings. The site fails to start, and the cause is a file nobody wrote.

      *** THE GENERATED FILENAME'S CASING DEPENDS ON WHETHER THE LEGACY FILE EXISTS, AND
          THAT MAKES THIS TASK'S OWN DELETION AN ESCALATION IF DONE ALONE. ***
      Measured A/B, both with this property absent:

          legacy Web.config on disk (pre-8.7)  -> generated "Web.config"  (capital W)
          legacy Web.config deleted  (8.7)     -> generated "web.config"  (lowercase)

      TransformWebConfig derives its output path from the project's own web.config item when
      one exists and falls back to the literal lowercase name when none does. So BEFORE this
      task the collision with Nop.Web's lowercase web.config was WINDOWS-ONLY (two names
      differing only in case - legal on Linux, one file on NTFS). DELETING Web.config, which is
      the other half of task 8.7, makes the generated name lowercase and therefore makes the
      collision EXACT ON EVERY FILESYSTEM, Linux included. Deleting the legacy file without
      also setting this property would have turned a platform-specific deployment defect into
      an unconditional one. Verified by publishing in both states.

      This is the same Web.config/web.config casing trap task 7.4 measured from the other
      side (runtime-deferrals.md section 31.2) - there it was TransformWebConfig's INPUT
      lookup being case-sensitive, here it is its OUTPUT path. Note task 8.1's comment on the
      publish-exclusion block predicted this file would be generated in lowercase; that was
      right only for the post-deletion state, and the pre-deletion state it was describing
      actually produced capital-W.

      IsTransformWebConfigDisabled is the SDK's documented switch for this. Verified after
      setting it: a publish of this project contains NO web.config at ANY casing, and the
      two-step publish leaves Nop.Web's own web.config byte-for-byte intact. Proven able to
      fail: with the property set back to false the generated shim reappears, naming
      Nop.Admin.dll.

      IsTransformWebConfigDisabled is the SDK's documented switch for this. Verified after
      setting it: a publish of this project contains NO web.config at ANY casing, and the
      two-step publish leaves Nop.Web's own web.config byte-for-byte intact.

      NO HAND-WRITTEN web.config REPLACES IT, and none is needed - confirmed rather than
      assumed. A web.config is only read by IIS, for the application it hosts; the hosted
      application is Nop.Web, whose src\Presentation\Nop.Web\web.config is the ANCM shim
      and carries the only two IIS settings 3.90 expressed for this tree. Nop.Admin never
      had a Global.asax and never was an application (3.90 recorded UseCustomServer=True
      with an external CustomServerUrl), so it has nothing to declare to IIS.
    -->
    <IsTransformWebConfigDisabled>true</IsTransformWebConfigDisabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
