    ===================================================================================
    TASK 8.2 - HOW THE Nop.Web HOST DISCOVERS THIS ASSEMBLY. READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING IT.
    ===================================================================================
    This target is one half of the answer; the other half is
    Nop.Web.Framework/Infrastructure/NopApplicationPartExtensions.cs. Together they replace
    two System.Web mechanisms that have no .NET counterpart:

      * System.Web's BuildManager loaded EVERY assembly in bin implicitly. In 3.90 this project
        set <OutputPath>..\bin\</OutputPath>, i.e. it dropped Nop.Admin.dll straight into
        Nop.Web\bin, and that is how the host came to have it at all. Design section 6 calls
        this out explicitly: "that packaging relationship is a build/deploy detail, not a
        compile-time project reference".
      * AreaRegistration.RegisterAllAreas() then found the area by reflecting over loaded
        assemblies. There is no equivalent; task 8.2's Infrastructure/RouteProvider.cs
        contributes the route through nopCommerce's own IRouteProvider instead.

    WHY NOT A ProjectReference FROM Nop.Web?  Considered and rejected, on three grounds:
      1. it converts design section 6's build/deploy relationship into a COMPILE-TIME one, so
         Nop.Web can no longer build on its own;
      2. it couples the 7.6 gate to the 8.8 gate - MSBuild builds project references first, so
         with Nop.Admin at ~3000 errors, Nop.Web (currently 0 errors / 15 warnings) would stop
         building entirely;
      3. the two projects are documented siblings and neither references the other, in either
         direction.

    WHY A POST-BUILD COPY AND NOT <OutputPath>..\bin\...</OutputPath>?  Two projects writing to
    one directory would let a clean or rebuild of either clobber the other's output, and
    "dotnet build Nop.Web" alone would appear to produce a half-populated directory. A copy is
    additive, directional (Nop.Admin -> Nop.Web, exactly as 3.90's OutputPath was), leaves this
    project's own bin\Debug\net10.0\ intact for the 8.8 gate, and cannot make Nop.Web fail.

    *** MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED, AND THE FIRST ANSWER WAS WRONG. ***
    A .NET 10 probe established that the copy ALONE is not sufficient, and that a naive copy was
    actively DANGEROUS. Nop.Core's WebAppTypeFinder is what scans loaded assemblies for
    IRouteProvider / IDependencyRegistrar / IStartupTask, and its inherited
    AppDomainTypeFinder.LoadMatchingAssemblies used AppDomain.Load(AssemblyName) - which on .NET
    binds from the host's deps.json-derived trusted-platform-assemblies list and does NOT probe
    the base directory. The probe put an assembly in the host's base directory but not in its
    deps.json and got

        System.IO.FileNotFoundException: Could not load file or assembly 'Nop.ProbeAdminLike ...'
          at System.AppDomain.Load(AssemblyName)
          at Nop.Core.Infrastructure.AppDomainTypeFinder.LoadMatchingAssemblies(String)

    and, because only BadImageFormatException was caught, that exception escaped
    WebAppTypeFinder.GetAssemblies() and TOOK THE HOST DOWN AT STARTUP. So dropping this
    assembly next to Nop.Web.dll without the accompanying Nop.Core fix would not merely have
    failed to load the admin - it would have stopped the storefront booting.

    Task 8.2 therefore also changed AppDomainTypeFinder.LoadMatchingAssemblies to load by PATH
    (AssemblyLoadContext.Default.LoadFromAssemblyPath) and to trace rather than throw. See the
    remarks on that method, and runtime-deferrals.md section 50.

    NOTE FOR TASK 18.x: this covers BUILD output only. "dotnet publish" of Nop.Web will not include
    Nop.Admin.dll, because publish is computed from Nop.Web's own item graph. Recorded as runtime
    deferral 8.2-2.

    TASK 8.5 MADE 8.2-2's OWN REMEDY - "publish both projects into one directory" - ACTUALLY WORK,
    and it did not before. Measured against the pre-8.5 layout, publishing Nop.Admin into Nop.Web's
    publish directory silently corrupted the storefront: this project's Content\ and Scripts\ landed
    at the publish ROOT, on top of Nop.Web's own trees, with 12 file collisions in Scripts\ and a
    structurally different Content\ionicons\ (storefront: ionicons.css at the top level; admin:
    css\ and fonts\ subdirectories). The Link metadata in the publish-shaping block above moves
    every content item under Administration\, which is BOTH collision-free AND the path the admin
    views' ~/Administration/Content|Scripts/... URLs require at runtime. Verified end to end:
    publish Nop.Web, then publish Nop.Admin into the same directory, and afterwards the storefront's
    Scripts\jquery.validate.min.js checksum is unchanged, Content\ionicons\ still holds only its own
    two files, the admin assets are all under Administration\, and Nop.Admin.dll sits at the root
    where WebAppTypeFinder scans. The assembly itself needs no Link - the root is where it belongs.
    What is still 18.x's is making that two-step a single deployment command.
  -->
  <Target Name="CopyNopAdminToHostOutput" AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(NopAdminCopyToHostOutput)' != 'false'">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <!-- ..\ is src\Presentation\Nop.Web\, i.e. the host project directory. -->
      <NopWebHostOutputDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\</NopWebHostOutputDir>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <ItemGroup>
      <NopAdminHostDrop Include="$(TargetPath)" />
      <NopAdminHostDrop Include="$(TargetDir)$(TargetName).pdb" Condition="Exists('$(TargetDir)$(TargetName).pdb')" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Only when the host has already been built. Creating the directory ourselves would leave a
      stray Nop.Admin.dll in a folder with no host to load it. ContinueOnError: a failed copy
      must not fail the 8.8 gate, which is a COMPILE gate.
    -->
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(NopAdminHostDrop)"
          DestinationFolder="$(NopWebHostOutputDir)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true"
          ContinueOnError="WarnAndContinue"
          Condition="Exists('$(NopWebHostOutputDir)')" />
    <Message Importance="high"
             Condition="!Exists('$(NopWebHostOutputDir)')"
             Text="Nop.Admin: the host output directory $(NopWebHostOutputDir) does not exist yet, so Nop.Admin.dll was NOT dropped into it. Build Nop.Web and then rebuild this project; without the drop the Admin area is not routable and its views do not resolve (task 8.2)." />
  </Target>

